  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy employs a genetic algorithm (GA) to evolve candidate h vectors representing the step function. Each h is a real-valued vector constrained to [0,1] with \u2211h = n/2. Crossovers and mutations generate new candidates, while a repair mechanism ensures constraint satisfaction. The GA aims to discover low-C\u2085 solutions by iteratively improving the population through fitness-based selection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, \u2211h = n/2\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k [\u03a3 h_i(1 - h_{i+k})] * (2/n)\n- Fitness function: Compute C\u2085 using np.correlate\n- Repair mechanism: Clips h to [0,1] and adjusts sum to n/2\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   1. Set n=100.\n   2. Start with population of 100 h vectors: 50 using initial_h_values, 50 random.\n   3. Each h is repaired to meet constraints.\n\nb. GA Iterations:\n   1. Compute fitness for each candidate.\n   2. Select top 50% as parents.\n   3. Generate offspring through crossover and mutation:\n      - Blend crossover with alpha=0.3\n      - Mutation: add Normal(0, 0.1) noise to 20% of genes\n   4. Repair offspring.\n   5. Replace weakest parents with offspring.\n   6. Track best h and its C\u2085.\n\nc. Termination:\n   1. Stop if budget exhausted or no improvement for 200 iterations.\n   2. Output best h found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Population size: 100 for balance between diversity and compute cost.\n- Crossover rate: 0.7\n- Mutation rate: 0.2\n- Repair mechanism ensures feasibility after each operation.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimate: 10000 iterations with 100 population members each, budget allows 1000 iterations.\nWithPath safeguarding to stop expensive operations and fallback to best h.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure h is feasible and track best C\u2085. Validate against evaluator.\nReturn best_h, C\u2085, and n_points.",
